AB  - Malaria is a significant global health problem with a substantial disease burden worldwide. In 2024, there were approximately 282 million cases of malaria, responsible for about 610,000 deaths, the majority on the African continent.[WHO. World Malaria Report 2025] Malaria results from infection with single-celled parasites belonging to the Plasmodium genus. The 6 species of Plasmodium that are known to cause disease in humans include P falciparum, P vivax, P ovale curtisi, P ovale wallikeri, P malariae, and P knowlesi. Relapsing malaria is caused by P vivax, P ovale curtisi, and P ovale wallikeri (the latter 2 are referred to as P ovale in much of this article). Globally, Plasmodium falciparum and Plasmodium vivax account for the majority of malaria cases. While Plasmodium falciparum is responsible for far more deaths, Plasmodium vivax is the most geographically widespread of all of the malaria species, can cause severe, even fatal infections, and resulted in an estimated 9.9 million cases in 2024. P ovale is less geographically widespread and much less common than P vivax, rarely causing severe disease.
